From: Ferrum Edge maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] frame: drain buffered bytes before propagating QUIC connection error

When a QUIC stack delivers stream data and a CONNECTION_CLOSE frame in
the same recv batch (common on Linux with batched recvmsg and coalesced
UDP datagrams), BufRecvStream::poll_read reports the connection error
while HEADERS / DATA bytes are still sitting in the local buffer. The
`?` propagation in FrameStream discards those bytes, so clients see a
connection error instead of the response that was already on the wire.

This patch defers ONLY connection-level errors
(StreamErrorIncoming::ConnectionErrorIncoming, decided by
FrameStreamError::is_connection_error). The deferred error is stored on
FrameStream (`pending_quic_error`, carried through `split()`):

  - poll_data keeps returning buffered body bytes, and poll_next keeps
    decoding buffered frames (for example trailers in the same chunk).
    The transport is not polled again while an error is pending.
  - The first poll_next or poll_data that finds nothing buffered
    returns the stored error and clears it, so it surfaces exactly once.
  - A connection error that truncates a DATA frame delivers the buffered
    part of the body and then the connection error, not UnexpectedEnd.

A peer RESET_STREAM (StreamTerminated), and every other stream-level
error, surfaces on the poll that reads it, ahead of buffered bytes, as
stock h3 does. Quinn reports a reset once and frees the stream, and RFC
9114 7.1 only makes a truncated frame a connection error when the stream
terminates cleanly. h3-quinn maps a read of rejected 0-RTT data
(ZeroRttRejected) and a read of a freed stream (ClosedStream) to
Unknown: deferring those would hand the application bytes the transport
has already disowned.

This diff is against h3 0.0.8, whose poll_next polls the transport
before it decodes, so poll_next can itself read the connection error
with a complete frame buffered and stores it the same way. Upstream
master decodes first (hyperium/h3#344); the semantics are the same.

Regression tests:
  - poll_next_drains_buffered_headers_before_quic_close
  - poll_next_drains_a_buffered_frame_when_it_reads_the_quic_close
  - poll_data_drains_buffered_body_before_quic_close
  - poll_data_surfaces_quic_close_over_a_truncated_buffered_body
  - poll_data_surfaces_stream_reset_over_a_truncated_buffered_body
  - poll_data_surfaces_stream_reset_over_a_buffered_frame
  - poll_next_surfaces_stream_reset_over_a_buffered_frame
  - poll_data_surfaces_unknown_stream_error_over_a_buffered_body
  - poll_next_surfaces_unknown_stream_error_over_a_buffered_frame

FakeRecv gains `chunk_then_error`, which makes the transport report an
error after its last chunk instead of end of stream, and a poll counter
so the tests can prove a stored error is surfaced without another
transport poll.

